orted by Saladino et al. (2001); plausible under evaporitic, mineral-catalyzed surface conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonolysis and hydrolysis of acetonitrile to glycine/alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetonitrile, ammonia, and formaldehyde generate aminonitrile and subsequently glycine and alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alternative pathway incorporating nitrile chemistry for amino acid formation; supported for other amino acids (Peltzer et al., 1984).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkylation of imidazole to imidazole-4-carbonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate mineral\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Alkylation at imidazole ring with HCN under UV and mineral catalysis to generate imidazole nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported for UV-induced ring alkylation in surface pools (Patel et al., 2015; speculative for this precise case, extends plausible chemistry).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of imidazole-4-carbonitrile to imidazole and urea\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"null\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mild heating\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the nitrile to the corresponding imidazole and release of urea group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotic hydrolysis of nitrile functional groups is thermodynamically favored; supported in analogous prebiotic scenarios (Yamada et al., 2014).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Histidine backbone build-up via condensation of imidazole and amino acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate mineral surface\", \"heating/drying\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"dry-down\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation between the nucleophilic imidazole, alpha-amino acid (alanine), and ammonia to form the imidazolyl-propanoic acid (histidine).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported in phosphorylation/mineral-catalyzed condensations on prebiotic phosphate surfaces (Kitadai et al., 2018; general surface dehydration logic).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of glycolaldehyde to aminoacetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS mineral\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8-9\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic attack of ammonia on glycolaldehyde to yield aminoacetaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported in hydrothermal amination reactions (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user, 1998).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of aminoacetaldehyde with formamidine to give imidazole\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyclization of diamino intermediate and imine (derived from HCN) gives imidazole ring.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotic imidazole formation via diamino/imine cyclization is supported in both surface and vent chemistry (Saladino et al., 2001; speculative but logical extension).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Addition of urea and glycolaldehyde for alternative imidazole synthesis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate mineral\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"repeated dry/damp cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Urea and glycolaldehyde condense, cyclize with HCN (as a C1/N source) under borate stabilization to give imidazole.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Borate minerals stabilize glycolaldehyde/urea chemistry (Kim et al., 2011, speculative for imidazole but supports related nucleobase assembly).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Classical C1 \u2192 C2 \u2192 imidazole \u2192 histidine\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde to glycolaldehyde to alanine to imidazole and then backbone