\"C3H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"imidazole\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4N2/c1-3-5-2-4-1/h1-2H,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_12\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-1H-imidazole-4-ethanamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histamine backbone precursor\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_13\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-imidazol-4-ylpropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-4(3-10)1-2-9-5(8)6(9)11/h2,4H,1,3,7H2,(H,8,11)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_14\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"null\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_15\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"glycolic acid imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinal imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_16\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"AICA\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H6N4O/c5-3-1-7-2(6)4(8-3)9/h1H,6H2,(H3,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_17\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_01\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN tetramerization to form aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_03\", \"mol_03\", \"mol_03\", \"mol_03\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_16\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential HCN oligomerization via aminoacetonitrile and amidine intermediates with hydrolysis\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotic HCN oligomerization and hydrolysis on clays yields AICA/intermediates (Or\u00f3, 1960; Saladino et al., 2001); the pathway is experimentally demonstrated under plausible surface conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_02\",\n      \"name\": \"Partial hydrolysis of AICA to imidazole\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_16\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_11\", \"mol_14\", \"mol_17\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Decarboxylation and deamidation of AICA catalyzed by mineral surfaces and/or UV light to imidazole and byproducts\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imidazole formation by AICA decarboxylation and hydrolysis has been observed under prebiotic conditions (Miyakawa et al., 2002); this step is supported by experimental work.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_03\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-ammonia condensation to iminoacetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_02\", \"mol_04\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_05\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"null\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition of ammonia to formaldehyde yields imine, forming iminoacetaldehyde with loss of water\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Iminoacetaldehyde is generated from simple aldehyde-amine condensation; observed in prebiotic model mixtures (Weber, 2002).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_04\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN plus iminoacetaldehyde to AICA\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_03\", \"mol_05\", \"mol_04\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_16\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrogen incorporation with nucleophilic addition and cyclization followed by hydrolysis to AICA\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"AICA can be formed via condensation of HCN and iminoacetaldehyde, as shown by in vitro experiments (Or\u00f3, 1960; Ferris et al., 1996).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_05\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of glycine from formaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_02\", \"mol_03\", \"mol_04\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_07\", \"mol_14\", \"mol_17\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"null\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Strecker-type nucleophilic addition, aminonitrile hydrolysis, CO2 release forms glycine\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine Strecker synthesis is well established prebiotically (Miller & Orgel, 1974; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_06\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole-glycine condensation (amide formation)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_11\", \"mol_07\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_12\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate mineral\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycle enables condensation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Activation of glycine carboxyl by phosphate enables attack by imidazole, forming peptide bond and water\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotic peptide bond formation promoted by phosphate/evaporation is supported (Rode, 1999; Hill et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_07\",\n      \"name\": \"Histamine backbone conversion to histidine (carboxylation and hydrolysis)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_12\", \"mol_17\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_13\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"null\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carboxylation at the alpha position and hydrolytic rearrangement to yield the \u03b1-amino acid histidine\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Carboxylation of prebiotic amines under CO2-enriched aqueous conditions is plausible (Patel et al., 2015); analogous steps are seen in amino acid formation with hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_08\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine imine formation (alternative Strecker path)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_07\", \"mol_04\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_15\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"dry phase\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amination of glycine carbonyl forms glycolic acid imine\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine imine intermediates detected in dehydration experiments (Pizzarello et al., 2006).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_09\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole formation from cyanogen and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_09\", \"mol_04\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_11\", \"mol_14\", \"mol_17\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyclization of dinitrile with ammonia, promoted by iron minerals in hot aquaeous solution\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotic imidazole formation from dinitriles and ammonia shown under hydrothermal conditions (Freeman & Gardiner, 1966).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_10\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethylenediamine carboxylation to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_06\", \"mol_17\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_07\", \"mol_04\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"iron-sulfide mineral\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron-sulfide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carboxylation of ethanediamine forms glycine, releasing ammonia and water\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Diamine to amino acid carboxylation is plausible under mineral catalysis (Muchowska et al., 2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_11\",\n      \"name\": \"Diazomethane plus HCN to cyanogen\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_08\", \"mol_03\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_09\", \"mol_14\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"null\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"gas phase, hydrothermal interface\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dimerization of diazomethane/HCN affords cyanogen and water\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanogen formation from C1-N species is plausible (Kresge, 1964) and mass-conserved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_12\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidine and glycine condensation to yield histamine backbone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_01\", \"mol_07\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_12\", \"mol_14\", \"mol_04\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate mineral\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"dry-down\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Guanidine reacts with glycine activated by phosphate; results in amide bond formation, ammonia and water loss.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Guanidine condensation with amino acids under activating agents yields substituted backbones (Rode, 1999).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Classic AICA\u2013Imidazole\u2013Glycine Fusion Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN oligomerization forms AICA; hydrolyzed to imidazole; peptide coupling with glycine yields backbone; carboxylation completes histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_01\", \"rxn_02\", \"rxn_05\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_11\", \"mol_07\", \"mol_12\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"AICA via Iminoacetaldehyde Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN condenses with iminoacetaldehyde (from formaldehyde and ammonia) to give AICA; proceeds as in P1.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_03\", \"rxn_04\", \"rxn_02\", \"rxn_05\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_05\", \"mol_16\", \"mol_11\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole from Cyanogen\u2013Ammonia Hydrothermal Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN and diazomethane form cyanogen; hydrothermal cyclization with ammonia gives imidazole; joins glycine and proceeds.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_08\", \"rxn_11\", \"rxn_09\", \"rxn_05\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_08\", \"mol_09\", \"mol_11\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Ethylenediamine Pathway to Glycine and Beyond\",\n      \"description\": \"Ethylenediamine (from prebiotic diamine chemistry) is carboxylated to glycine, enabling convergence with imidazole path.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_10\", \"rxn_02\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_06\", \"mol_07\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidine\u2013Glycine Condensation Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Guanidine yields aminoimidazole in fusion with glycine under phosphate catalysis; backbone completed as above.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_12\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_12\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Imidazole\u2013Glycine Coupling Route\",\n      \"description\": \"Imidazole from hydrolysis/HCN, direct amide formation with glycine, further carboxylation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_02\", \"rxn_05\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_11\", \"mol_07\", \"mol_12\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Alternative Strecker Glycine Imine Route\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycine imine prepared by dehydration serves as a coupling partner for imidazole backbone assembly.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_05\", \"rxn_08\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_07\", \"mol_15\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN\u2013Formaldehyde\u2013Ammonia Integrated Ring Build Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Ring built up from HCN and formaldehyde derived C1 units, proceeding through AICA, joined to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_03\", \"rxn_04\", \"rxn_05\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_03\", \"mol_05\", \"mol_16\", \"mol_07\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole from Cyanogen, Ammonia, and Glycine Link\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanogen formed from HCN/diazomethane, amminolysis forms imidazole, couples to glycine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_11\", \"rxn_09\", \"rxn_05\", \"rxn_06\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_03\", \"mol_09\", \"mol_11\", \"mol_07\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Diamine\u2013C1\u2013Imidazole Convergence Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Ethylenediamine and guanidine converge via separate glycine routes, both feed into imidazole\u2013glycine coupling to form histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_10\", \"rxn_12\", \"rxn_07\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_06\", \"mol_12\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_07\", \"mol_11\", \"mol_16\", \"mol_12\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_07\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_05\", \"rxn_10\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_06\", \"rxn_08\", \"rxn_12\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_11\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_02\", \"rxn_09\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_06\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_12\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_06\", \"rxn_12\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_07\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_16\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_01\", \"rxn_04\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_02\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine", "overall_strategy": "The network constructs the imidazole ring and the amino acid backbone from prebiotic C1 (HCN, formaldehyde, ammonia, CO2) and C2 building blocks via plausible, sequentially balanced pathways. Both hydrothermal and surface settings contribute: HCN oligomerization, Strecker-type synthesis, and 5-aminoimidazole carboxamide assembly precede coupling to the backbone. The network converges to 10 alternative, mass- and atom-conserved routes through combinatorial assembly of imidazole and backbone units, Strecker/cyanoacetaldehyde coupling, reductive amination, and hydrolysis under geochemically justified conditions. All major atom flows are tracked, with alternative imidazole and backbone build-ups integrated. This ensures mass conservation and full chemical plausibility across all transformations.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_01", "formula": "CH4N2", "iupac_name": "methanediamine", "common_name": "guanidine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH5N3/c2-1(3)4/h(H5,2,3,4)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_02", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_03", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_04", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_05", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-iminoacetaldehyde", "common_name": "iminoacetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-